    "available_at": "2026-10-30T01:27:09.208688+00:00",
    "body": "Amazon reported record operating profitability in the third quarter on Thursday after the market closed, as the e-commerce giant continued expanding its delivery network and robotics investments amid higher transportation costs.\nCEO Andy Jassy said investments in same-day fulfillment facilities and ultra-fast delivery services are helping the company attract more customers while lowering fulfillment expenditures.\n“We’re on track to reduce cost to serve per unit for the fourth consecutive year,” Jassy said during a call with analysts.\nThe Seattle-based company reported third-quarter revenue of $201.9 billion, up 12% from $180.2 billion a year earlier. Operating income surged 61% to $28.1 billion, compared with $17.4 billion in the same period last year.\nRevenue in the quarter came in below Wall Street estimates of $202.3 billion, while earnings per share came in at $1.89, lower than the expectation of $1.96.\nAmazon’s (Nasdaq: AMZN ) operating margin reached 13.9%, its highest ever, as improvements across its retail and cloud computing businesses helped offset higher expenses.\n“In our fulfillment network, the team navigated higher transportation costs from fuel inflation and higher line haul rates,” Chief Financial Officer Brian Olsavsky said.\nDespite those pressures, Amazon lowered its overall cost to serve during the quarter through better inventory placement, shorter shipping distances and package consolidation, he said.\nShipping costs increased 11% year over year, compared with 12% growth in units shipped. Excluding fuel and linehaul inflation, shipping costs rose 8%, approximately 4 percentage points slower than unit growth.\nAmazon’s North America segment, which includes its U.S. and Canadian retail operations, generated $112.9 billion in third-quarter revenue, up 6% year over year.\nOperating income in the segment jumped to $8.7 billion from $4.8 billion a year earlier, an increase of approximately 81%.\nNorth America’s operating margin reached 7.7%.\nInternational revenue increased 5% to $43.1 billion, while operating income climbed to $1.6 billion from $1.2 billion.\nAmazon said the timing of Prime Day affected year-over-year comparisons because the shopping event occurred in the second quarter of 2026 for most major markets, compared with the third quarter in 2025.\nExcluding that timing change, Amazon estimated third-quarter revenue growth would have been approximately 4 percentage points higher.\nAmazon said it achieved its fastest third-quarter delivery speeds ever for Prime members, delivering more than 1.5 billion items on the same day or overnight during the three-month period ending Sept. 30.\nAutomation remains a major component of Amazon’s strategy to lower fulfillment expenses and increase productivity.\nJassy told analysts the company now operates more than 1 million robots across its fulfillment network.\nHe said robotics has contributed to improved productivity, reduced operating costs and record-low safety incident levels across its fulfillment operations.\nAmazon doubled its fleet of robotic arms, including Cardinal and Sparrow systems, during 2026, according to Olsavsky.\nThe company is also developing robotics foundation models designed to help machines perform increasingly complex physical tasks.\nDuring the question-and-answer portion of Thursday’s earnings call, Jassy said that transportation cost pressures are expected to continue into the fourth quarter.\nResponding to an analyst’s question about fuel and linehaul inflation, Jassy said the impact would be somewhat higher in the fourth quarter than in the third.\nAmazon implemented a fuel surcharge earlier this year for sellers using its Fulfillment by Amazon service, allowing the company to recover some of its higher fuel expenses.\nHowever, Jassy said the surcharge does not fully offset the increases, leaving Amazon exposed to higher costs for inbound retail shipments and outbound deliveries.\nAmazon expects fourth-quarter net sales between $240 billion and $247 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 12% to 16%.\nThe company forecast fourth-quarter operating income between $28.5 billion and $33.5 billion, compared with $25 billion in the year-earlier period.\nAmazon Q3 2026: Key financial and operating metrics\nThe table below is suitable for inserting after the article’s opening financial results section.\nMetric | Q3 2025 | Q3 2026 | YoY change\nTotal revenue | $180.17B | $201.94B | +12.1%\nOperating income | $17.42B | $28.08B | +61.2%\nOperating margin | 9.7% | 13.9% | +4.2 pts\nNet income | $21.19B | $20.69B | -2.4%\nDiluted EPS | $1.95 | $1.89 | -3.1%\nNorth America revenue | $106.27B | $112.85B | +6.2%\nNorth America operating income | $4.79B | $8.72B | +82.1%\nInternational revenue | $40.90B | $43.11B | +5.4%\nAWS revenue | $33.01B | $45.98B | +39.3%\nWorldwide shipping costs | $25.38B | $28.30B | +11.5%\nFulfillment expenses | $27.68B | $30.00B | +8.4%\nThird-party seller services revenue | $42.49B | $46.49B | +9.4%\nOnline store revenue | $67.41B | $68.47B | +1.6%\nWorldwide paid-unit growth | 11% | 12% | +1 pt\nSource: Amazon Q3 2026 earnings release.\nWhy it matters: Amazon’s ability to lower per-unit delivery costs despite higher fuel and linehaul expenses illustrates how automation, inventory placement and transportation network optimization could reshape the economics of e-commerce fulfillment and parcel delivery.",
